message:
{{#is_alert}}The PR agent's p95 cost per review over the last day is over $1.00 — the retired single loop's range (48c–$1.10 per review, RC1-393); the pipeline's reference PRs price at 6–21c (RC1-427). A review ran away: read its trace.{{/is_alert}}
{{#is_warning}}p95 cost per review over the last day is over $0.50 — more than twice the pipeline's largest reference PR (21c, RC1-427) and well above the production p50 of ~4c; worth reading the trace for the most expensive review.{{/is_warning}}

This is the per-*review* signal (RC1-395). The fleet's per-call monitor (RC1-377) measures something else: the pipeline is four or five short calls per review (warm call, three reviewers, verifier), so cost per call is small whether or not the review got cheaper. One `pr_agent.review.cost_usd` point per review, priced at the end of the trace from all four token counts (cache writes 1.25x, reads 0.1x).

Triage on the [fleet dashboard](https://app.datadoghq.com/dashboard/bwm-uny-qqs), "Cost per review" group:
- **by repo**: a review above 50c is a very large PR (the prefix write scales with the diff and the context block) or a runaway; the stage costs on the trace say which.
- One repo carrying the p95 is a large PR, not a regression.
- The [LLM Obs trace](https://app.datadoghq.com/llm/traces) for the review carries `cost_usd` and `stage_cost_usd.<stage>` on its `pr_review` workflow span.

Thresholds were set from the RC1-393 record (2026-09-06: scout 18–45c, single loop 48c–$1.10). Since RC1-422/427 (2026-09-12) the only path is the no-scout pipeline at 6–21c on the reference PRs; the thresholds are now generous. Re-derive when the model or the corpus changes.

